from common import *
mc('S2.1','sl','Charge balance in a fertilizer','Which formula represents ammonium phosphate?', ['NH₄PO₄','(NH₄)₂PO₄','(NH₄)₃PO₄','NH₄(PO₄)₃'],2,'Three NH₄⁺ ions balance one PO₄³⁻ ion. The brackets apply the subscript to the entire ammonium ion.')
mc('S2.1','sl','Conductivity during melting','A solid ionic compound does not conduct electricity, but its melt does. What changes on melting?', ['Electrons become delocalized throughout the sample','The ions become able to move through the sample','The ions lose their charges','Covalent molecules replace the ions'],1,'The charged ions already exist in the solid but are fixed in lattice positions. They can transport charge in the liquid.')
mc('S2.1','sl','Comparing ionic attractions','Use only charge and ionic-size arguments. Which pair is expected to have the strongest electrostatic attraction? The ion-separation distances are supplied.', ['+1 and −1, 250 pm','+2 and −2, 210 pm','+2 and −2, 280 pm','+1 and −2, 210 pm'],1,'The largest charge product and shortest separation give the strongest electrostatic attraction: +2/−2 at 210 pm.')
mc('S2.2','sl','Bonding in ammonium',r'When $\mathrm{NH_3}$ reacts with $\mathrm{H^+}$ to form $\mathrm{NH_4^+}$, where do the two electrons in the newly formed bond originate?', ['One from N and one from H⁺','Both from N','Both from H⁺','From a neighbouring water molecule in every case'],1,'The nitrogen lone pair is donated to H⁺. Both bonding electrons originate from nitrogen; the four N–H bonds in the resulting ion are equivalent.')
mc('S2.2','sl','Predicting molecular polarity','Which molecule has polar bonds but no permanent molecular dipole in its ideal geometry?', ['H₂S','CH₂Cl₂','CO₂','NH₃'],2,'CO₂ is linear and symmetrical, so the C–O bond dipoles cancel. The other listed molecules have non-cancelling bond dipoles.')
mc('S2.2','sl','Hydrogen bonding in a pure liquid','Which pure substance forms hydrogen bonds between its own molecules?', ['CH₃OCH₃','CH₃CH₂NH₂','CH₃CH₂CH₃','CH₃Cl'],1,'Ethylamine has N–H bonds and a nitrogen lone pair. Dimethyl ether can accept hydrogen bonds from water but lacks an O–H or N–H donor in the pure liquid.')
mc('S2.2','ahl','Electron domains in an ion',r'What is the molecular geometry of $\mathrm{ICl_4^-}$?', ['Tetrahedral','Square planar','Trigonal planar','Seesaw'],1,'There are six electron domains: four bonding pairs and two opposite lone pairs. Octahedral electron-domain geometry gives a square-planar molecular geometry.')
mc('S2.2','ahl','Bond inventory in a nitrile',r'How many $\sigma$ and $\pi$ bonds are present in $\mathrm{CH_3C\equiv N}$?', ['4 σ and 3 π','5 σ and 2 π','6 σ and 1 π','3 σ and 4 π'],1,'Three C–H bonds and the C–C bond are σ bonds; C≡N contains one σ and two π bonds. Total: five σ and two π.')
mc('S2.2','ahl','Hybridization at a carbonyl','What is the hybridization of the carbonyl carbon in ethanal, CH₃CHO?', ['sp','sp²','sp³','It cannot be described without d orbitals'],1,'The carbonyl carbon has three electron domains and a trigonal-planar σ framework. One unhybridized p orbital forms the π bond.')
mc('S2.3','sl','Why an alloy is harder','Why can adding differently sized atoms to a metal increase its hardness?', ['It removes every delocalized electron','It makes all metal atoms into neutral molecules','It disrupts regular layers and makes sliding more difficult','It necessarily creates an ionic lattice'],2,'Differently sized atoms distort the metal lattice and obstruct layer movement. Metallic bonding and mobile electrons remain.')
mc('S2.4','sl','Reading an addition polymer','An addition polymer has repeating unit –CH₂–CHCl–. Which monomer forms it?', ['CH₃CH₂Cl','CH₂=CHCl','CHCl=CHCl','CH₂ClCH₂Cl'],1,'The C=C bond in chloroethene opens to create the carbon-chain backbone. No small molecule is eliminated.')
mc('S2.4','ahl','Identifying a condensation link','Which linkage is characteristic of a polyamide?', ['–C(=O)–O–','–O–O–','–C(=O)–NH–','–C=C–'],2,'A polyamide contains repeated amide links, –C(=O)–NH–, formed between suitable carboxylic-acid and amine groups.')
wr('S2.1','sl','Classifying three solids','The observations below are constructed.'+table(['Solid','Melting behaviour','Electrical behaviour'],[('A','High melting point','Conducts only when molten'),('B','High melting point','Conducts as solid and liquid'),('C','Low melting point','Does not conduct in either state')]),[('Identify the most likely bonding model for A, B and C.',[('A1','A: ionic; B: metallic.'),('A1','C: simple molecular covalent.')]),('Explain the conductivity contrast between solid A and solid B.',[('R1','In A, ions are fixed in the lattice, so they cannot carry charge through the solid.'),('R1','In B, delocalized electrons are mobile and carry charge.')])],kind='data-based',difficulty='medium')
wr('S2.1','sl','Naming and dissolving an ionic salt',r'A salt contains only $\mathrm{Ca^{2+}}$ and $\mathrm{NO_3^-}$ ions.',[('Deduce its formula and write an equation for its dissolution in water.',[('A1','Calcium nitrate: Ca(NO₃)₂.'),('A1',r'$\mathrm{Ca(NO_3)_2(s)\rightarrow Ca^{2+}(aq)+2NO_3^-(aq)}$.')]),('Explain why its aqueous solution conducts electricity and why conductivity alone cannot establish the salt’s identity.',[('R1','The dissolved ions move through the solution and transport charge.'),('R1','Many dissolved ionic compounds produce mobile ions, so the observation is not chemically specific.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S2.2','sl','Two sulfur compounds',r'Consider $\mathrm{H_2S}$ and $\mathrm{CS_2}$. Use the usual Lewis structures.',[('State the electron-domain geometry, molecular geometry and approximate bond angle around S in H₂S using the four-domain VSEPR model.',[('A1','Four electron domains give tetrahedral electron-domain geometry; the molecule is bent.'),('A1','The H–S–H angle is less than 109.5°. Accept an approximate measured angle near 92°; do not require the water angle.')]),('Explain why CS₂ has no net dipole while H₂S does.',[('R1','Linear, symmetrical CS₂ has cancelling bond dipoles.'),('R1','The bent geometry of H₂S prevents its bond dipoles cancelling.')])],figure=molecule([(180,160,'S'),(360,160,'C'),(540,160,'S')],[(0,1,2),(1,2,2)],'CS₂ connectivity'))
wr('S2.2','sl','Carbon allotropes as electrodes','Graphite and diamond are both carbon solids with high melting temperatures.',[('Explain why graphite conducts electricity while diamond does not.',[('R1','Each carbon in graphite forms three σ bonds, leaving electrons delocalized across the layers.'),('R1','In diamond each carbon forms four covalent bonds, leaving no mobile delocalized electrons.')]),('Explain the high melting temperatures of both solids.',[('R1','Both are extended covalent networks.'),('R1','Melting requires disruption of many strong covalent bonds, demanding substantial energy.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S2.2','sl','Boiling-point evidence','The boiling temperatures are rounded experimental values.'+table(['Substance','Molar mass / g mol⁻¹','Boiling temperature / °C'],[('Ethane',30,-89),('Methanol',32,65),('Ethanol',46,78)]),[('Explain the much higher boiling temperature of methanol than ethane.',[('R1','Methanol molecules form hydrogen bonds whereas ethane molecules interact through London forces only.'),('R1','Stronger intermolecular attractions require more energy to overcome; covalent O–H bonds are not broken during boiling.')]),('Explain why ethanol boils at a higher temperature than methanol even though both form hydrogen bonds.',[('R1','Ethanol has a larger electron cloud and greater polarizability.'),('R1','Its stronger London forces add to the hydrogen bonding.')])],kind='data-based',difficulty='medium')
wr('S2.2','sl','Solvent choice in chromatography','A mixture of two neutral organic compounds is separated on polar silica. In a weakly polar solvent, both spots remain close to the baseline.',[('Suggest a change to improve movement of the spots and explain its effect.',[('A1','Increase the polarity of the mobile phase.'),('R1','The mobile phase competes more strongly with the analytes for the polar stationary phase and can carry them further.')]),('Explain why comparing Rf values from different solvent mixtures is unreliable for identifying a compound.',[('R1','Rf depends on the balance of interactions with both phases.'),('R1','Changing solvent composition changes those interactions, so an identity comparison requires the same conditions and ideally a co-run standard.')])])
wr('S2.2','ahl','Formal charge in cyanide',r'For $\mathrm{CN^-}$, use a C≡N triple bond with one lone pair on each atom. Carbon has four and nitrogen five valence electrons.',[('Calculate the formal charge on each atom.',[('M1','Formal charge=valence electrons−non-bonding electrons−half the bonding electrons.'),('A1','C: 4−2−3=−1; N: 5−2−3=0.')]),('Determine the number of σ and π bonds, and the hybridization of carbon in this model.',[('A1','One σ bond and two π bonds.'),('A1','Carbon is sp hybridized: one hybrid orbital participates in the σ bond and the other contains the lone pair.')])])
wr('S2.2','ahl','Five domains, two lone pairs',r'The central chlorine in $\mathrm{ClF_3}$ has three bonding pairs and two lone pairs.',[('Predict the electron-domain geometry and molecular geometry.',[('A1','Trigonal-bipyramidal electron-domain geometry.'),('A1','T-shaped molecular geometry.')]),('Explain why the lone pairs occupy equatorial positions in the VSEPR model.',[('R1','An equatorial position has two 90° interactions, compared with three for an axial position.'),('R1','Putting the more strongly repelling lone pairs equatorial minimizes close repulsions.')])])
wr('S2.2','ahl','Ozone and bond equivalence',r'Ozone has two equivalent O–O bonds, intermediate in length between typical single and double O–O bonds.',[('Describe the resonance model that explains these observations.',[('R1','Two contributing Lewis structures place the double bond on alternate sides of the central oxygen.'),('R1','The real molecule has delocalized bonding and equivalent O–O bonds; it does not alternate between the structures.')]),('For either contributing structure, state the formal charges on the central O and the singly bonded terminal O.',[('A1','Central O: +1.'),('A1','Singly bonded terminal O: −1; the doubly bonded terminal O is neutral.')])])
wr('S2.2','ahl','Counting bonds in an ester',r'The connectivity of methyl ethanoate is shown. Each CH₃ group has three C–H bonds.',[('Determine the total numbers of σ and π bonds.',[('M1','Count six C–H σ bonds plus four heavy-atom σ connections.'),('A1','10 σ bonds and 1 π bond.')]),('State the hybridization and approximate bond angles at the carbonyl carbon, and explain the restriction on rotation about its C=O bond.',[('A1','sp²; approximately 120°.'),('R1','Rotation would destroy the sideways p-orbital overlap of the π bond and therefore requires breaking that component of the bond.')])],figure=molecule([(120,170,'CH₃'),(300,170,'C'),(300,80,'O'),(470,170,'O'),(620,170,'CH₃')],[(0,1,1),(1,2,2),(1,3,1),(3,4,1)]))
wr('S2.3','sl','Comparing metallic bonds','Sodium and magnesium are neighbouring period-3 metals. Magnesium has a much higher melting temperature.',[('Explain the difference using the metallic bonding model.',[('R1','Each Mg atom supplies two delocalized electrons and forms a 2+ metal ion in the model, compared with one electron and Na⁺.'),('R1','Mg ions are smaller / have higher charge density, producing stronger attraction between positive ions and delocalized electrons.')]),('Explain why a sheet of magnesium can be bent without the bonding model requiring alternating positive and negative layers.',[('R1','Metallic attraction is non-directional.'),('R1','Positive-ion layers can shift while remaining attracted to the mobile electron sea.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S2.3','ahl','A transition-metal conductor','A student models a transition metal using only one delocalized s electron per atom.',[('Explain why this model may underestimate metallic bond strength in transition metals.',[('R1','d electrons as well as s electrons can contribute to delocalized metallic bonding.'),('R1','A greater density of participating electrons can strengthen attraction to the positive metal centres.')]),('Explain why high electrical conductivity does not itself show that a metal has weak metallic bonds.',[('R1','Conductivity depends on the mobility of delocalized charge carriers.'),('R1','Bond strength concerns the energy needed to separate the solid; mobile electrons can coexist with strong metallic attraction.')])])
wr('S2.4','sl','Designing a cable sheath',r'Poly(ethene) is produced from ethene.',[('Draw two consecutive repeating units, displaying the carbon backbone and all attached hydrogens.',[('A1',r'$-\mathrm{CH_2-CH_2-CH_2-CH_2}-$, with continuation bonds at both ends.'),('A1','All backbone C–C bonds are single; each backbone carbon has two hydrogens.')]),('Explain why the polymer can be an electrical insulator despite containing many bonds.',[('R1','Its electrons are localized in covalent bonds and it has no mobile ions or delocalized charge carriers.')]),('Predict how increased chain branching generally affects packing and density.',[('R1','Branching prevents close packing, generally reducing density and crystallinity relative to less-branched chains.')])])
wr('S2.4','sl','Recognizing a substituted monomer',r'A polymer has repeating unit $[-\mathrm{CH_2-C(CH_3)(Cl)}-]_n$.',[('Draw or write the structural formula of the monomer and classify the polymerization.',[('A1',r'$\mathrm{CH_2=C(CH_3)Cl}$.'),('A1','Addition polymerization; the C=C π bond is consumed to form the chain.')]),('Explain why no HCl is lost for each repeating unit.',[('R1','The substituents remain attached to the same carbon atoms.'),('R1','Addition polymerization retains all monomer atoms; it does not form the chain by eliminating a small molecule.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S2.4','ahl','A polyester from two monomers',r'A polyester is made from $\mathrm{HOCH_2CH_2OH}$ and $\mathrm{HOOCCH_2COOH}$.',[('Write one repeating unit with continuation bonds.',[('A1',r'$[-\mathrm{OCH_2CH_2O-C(=O)-CH_2-C(=O)}-]_n$.'),('A1','Correct ester linkages and continuation through the terminal carbonyl carbon and initial oxygen; an equivalent reversed unit is accepted.')]),('State the eliminated small molecule and explain why both monomers must be bifunctional to form long chains.',[('A1','Water.'),('R1','Each monomer needs two reactive groups so that it can connect to two neighbouring units rather than terminate the chain.')])])
wr('S2.4','ahl','Recovering polyamide monomers',r'A polyamide contains the repeat $[-\mathrm{NH(CH_2)_4NH-C(=O)(CH_2)_3C(=O)}-]_n$.',[('Deduce the two monomers used in a condensation route employing a diamine and a dicarboxylic acid.',[('A1',r'$\mathrm{H_2N(CH_2)_4NH_2}$.'),('A1',r'$\mathrm{HOOC(CH_2)_3COOH}$.')]),('Explain why hydrolysis breaks the chain and identify the atoms added across each cleaved amide link when neutral monomers are represented.',[('R1','Hydrolysis breaks a backbone amide C–N bond, dividing the long chain.'),('A1','OH is added to the carbonyl carbon and H to nitrogen, restoring a carboxylic-acid group and an amine group.')])])
wr('S2.4','sl','The bonding triangle','Two hypothetical binary solids have the following descriptors. Electronegativity averages and differences are dimensionless.'+table(['Solid','Mean electronegativity','Electronegativity difference'],[('P',1.1,0.2),('Q',2.1,2.0)]),[('Use the bonding-continuum model to suggest the dominant bonding character of P and Q.',[('A1','P: metallic character; both atoms have relatively low electronegativity and a small difference.'),('A1','Q: ionic character; the difference is large.')]),('Explain why these descriptors should not be interpreted as an exact percentage of ionic bonding.',[('R1','Bonding models describe a continuum and the position is a qualitative guide.'),('R1','Properties also depend on structure and composition; the descriptors are not a calibrated percentage-ionic-character measurement.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('S2.2','ahl','A resonance energy comparison',r'Complete hydrogenation of a hypothetical localized three-double-bond ring is predicted to release $360\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Hydrogenation of benzene releases $208\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$.',[('Calculate the energetic stabilization of benzene relative to the localized model.',[('M1','Compare the magnitudes of the two hydrogenation enthalpies.'),('A1','360−208=152 kJ mol⁻¹; benzene lies lower in energy by this amount relative to that model.')]),('State two structural observations explained by delocalization.',[('A1','All six C–C bonds have equal length.'),('A1','The C–C bond lengths are intermediate between typical single and double bonds; accept planar carbon framework with overlapping p orbitals as an alternative structural feature.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('S2.2','ahl','Six domains with one lone pair',r'Consider $\mathrm{BrF_5}$, with five Br–F bonds and one lone pair on bromine.',[('State its electron-domain geometry and molecular geometry.',[('A1','Octahedral electron-domain geometry.'),('A1','Square-pyramidal molecular geometry.')]),('Explain why the molecule is polar although all five ligands are fluorine.',[('R1','The Br–F bonds are polar.'),('R1','The square-pyramidal geometry is not fully symmetrical; the bond dipoles do not cancel because the position opposite the axial F is occupied by a lone pair.')])])
wr('S2.2','sl','Dry and wet molecular liquids','Pure ethanol has very low electrical conductivity. A solution of sodium chloride in water conducts well.',[('Explain the contrast in charge carriers.',[('R1','Ethanol consists mainly of neutral molecules and has very few mobile ions.'),('R1','Dissolved NaCl supplies mobile Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.')]),('Explain why ethanol and water mix, referring to interactions between unlike molecules.',[('R1','The ethanol hydroxy group and water molecules can form hydrogen bonds with each other.'),('R1','These favourable interactions permit mixing; hydrogen bonding is not the transfer of an electron to create an ionic compound.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S2.2','ahl','A triple bond and a double bond',r'For $\mathrm{HC\equiv C-CH=CH_2}$, carbon atoms are numbered from the left.',[('State the hybridization of carbons 1–4 and count all π bonds.',[('A1','C1 and C2: sp; C3 and C4: sp².'),('A1','Three π bonds in total: two in the triple bond and one in the double bond.')]),('State the approximate C1–C2–C3 angle and C2–C3–C4 angle, explaining the difference.',[('A1','Approximately 180° at C2 and 120° at C3.'),('R1','C2 has two electron domains; C3 has three. Multiple bonds count as one domain for VSEPR.')])],figure=molecule([(110,160,'HC'),(280,160,'C'),(450,160,'CH'),(620,160,'CH₂')],[(0,1,3),(1,2,1),(2,3,2)]))
wr('S2.1','sl','Inferring charge from a crystal',r'An ionic compound contains metal ions $\mathrm{M^{3+}}$ and oxide ions. It has a high melting point and is brittle.',[('Deduce its simplest formula and explain the high melting point.',[('A1','M₂O₃, because 2(+3)+3(−2)=0.'),('R1','Strong electrostatic attractions between oppositely charged ions throughout the lattice require substantial energy to overcome.')]),('Use the lattice model to explain brittleness.',[('R1','A displacement can bring like-charged ions into adjacent positions.'),('R1','Repulsion between them causes the lattice to split rather than allowing sustained sliding.')])],difficulty='medium')
save('S2')
